//? Title: Serving Exported Weights with a Dynamic Batch
//? Subtitle: Native least squares fits the coefficients; C++ supplies and checks the serving function
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A fitted model must accept new batch sizes after leaving its training environment. We fit a
small affine model with native least squares, export its Float32 weights, and evaluate it in C++ on two batch
sizes. The native process needs the numeric weights and matching inference code; it does not
load a TensorFlow SavedModel directory.
)
\toc

> The method

Serving separates parameter estimation from evaluation. Once weights W and b are fixed,
Y=XW+b has shape [batch,output_width], and batch may vary between calls. The native helper
requires floating inputs, weights and bias of the same dtype and compatible dimensions.
The fixture fits the full-rank design Z=[1,X] by least squares:

$$
\widehat\theta=\arg\min_\theta\|Z\theta-y\|_2^2,\qquad y=.75x_0-1.5x_1+.25.
$$
\alttext(Estimate an intercept and two slopes from a full-rank training design.)

Native NPZ serialization exports learned coefficients, not executable graph functions or SignatureDefs. The C++
consumer retains the affine computation and performs explicit shape/dtype validation. NoGradGuard
inside affine_inference avoids retaining an inference graph. Dense evaluation costs
O(batch times input_width times output_width). Matching outputs on a few queries is a useful
contract check, not proof of generalization to another population.

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/saved_model_inference_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/saved_model_inference_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/saved_model_inference_concept.tikz > build/saved_model_inference_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/saved_model_inference_concept.svg build/saved_model_inference_concept.dvi > build/saved_model_inference_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/saved_model_inference_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered build/saved_model_inference_concept.svg from local TikZ source.
// \image(figures/saved_model_inference_concept.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Native least squares fits the coefficients; C++ supplies and checks the serving function)
\alttext(Native fit; least squares coefficients Then Export Float32 NPZ; weights + bias Then C++ serving contract; dynamic batch; no grad.)

> Simple examples

```{cpp, results="output", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/array_io.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const auto x=n::tensor({0.,0.,1.,0.,0.,1.,2.,-3.,-1.,2.},{5,2},n::DType::Float64);
 const auto y=n::matmul(x,n::tensor({.75,-1.5},{2,1},n::DType::Float64))+.25;
 const auto z=n::concat({n::ones({5,1},n::DType::Float64),x},1);
 const auto coef=n::lstsq(z,y,0.,false);
 n::save_npz("build/saved_model_inference_weights.npz",{{"weights",n::slice_axis(coef,0,1,3).to(n::DType::Float32)},{"bias",n::slice_axis(coef,0,0,1).reshape({1}).to(n::DType::Float32)}});
 if(n::max(n::abs(n::matmul(z,coef)-y)).item<double>()>=1e-12)return 1;
 std::print("Native least squares recovered intercept .25 and slopes .75, -1.5; exported Float32 arrays.\n");
}
```
// result_begin:
// Native least squares recovered intercept .25 and slopes .75, -1.5; exported Float32 arrays.
// result_end

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/saved_model_inference_agreement.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;n::ParameterStore s;s.load("build/saved_model_inference_weights.npz",false);
 const auto w=s.at("weights"),b=s.at("bias"),x=n::tensor({{0,0},{1,0},{0,1},{2,-3}}),y=n::affine_inference(x,w,b);
 const auto single=n::affine_inference(n::tensor({{2,-3}}),w,b);
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.lines({.25,1,-1.25,6.25},y.to_vector<double>(),"native output")
 .title("Exported model agrees with its generating affine rule").x_label("analytic prediction").y_label("native prediction");
 p.size(820,420).save_svg("build/saved_model_inference_agreement.svg");
 if(y.to_vector<double>()!=std::vector<double>({.25,1,-1.25,6.25})||single.item()!=6.25||y.requires_grad())return 1;
 std::print("Batch shapes: {} and {}; predictions: {}.\n",n::shape_str(y.shape()),n::shape_str(single.shape()),y.to_vector<double>());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Batch shapes: [4, 1] and [1, 1]; predictions: [0.25, 1, -1.25, 6.25].
\image(figures/saved_model_inference_agreement.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The same weights serve four rows or one row without rebuilding a graph.)
\alttext(The same weights serve four rows or one row without rebuilding a graph.)

> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;int bad=0;const auto w=n::tensor({.75,-1.5},{2,1}),b=n::tensor({.25});
 try{n::affine_inference(n::zeros({1,3}),w,b);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++bad;}
 try{n::affine_inference(n::zeros({1,2},n::DType::Float64),w,b);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++bad;}
 if(bad!=2)return 1;std::print("Incompatible input width and dtype rejected: {}.\n",bad);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Incompatible input width and dtype rejected: 2.
// result_end


> Conclusion

Native estimation and serving inference agree across dynamic batch sizes, and incompatible inputs
fail at the serving boundary. This is weight interoperability with explicit native code. It does
not demonstrate SavedModel loading, graph portability or execution of arbitrary exported functions.

> References

- [TensorFlow SavedModel guide: functions and signatures](https://www.tensorflow.org/guide/saved_model).
- [NumPy NPY format specification](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.lib.format.html).
